      "math_academy_id": "e-128",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x) = x^2,$ what is $f'(x)$ according to the definition of the derivative?",
      "worked_solution": "The derivative $f '(x)$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} .\n$$\n\nSince $f(x) = x^2$,  we have that $f(x+h) = (x+h)^2.$ Hence,  we get\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f'(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{(x+h)^2-x^2}{h} .\n$$",
